  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 218 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nAn upper level trof and associated cold front move eastward out of \nthe Rockies into the High Plains Wednesday. Winds will increase with \nthe frontal passage and compressional heating with downslope flow \nwill warm temperatures briefly into the 60s before the frontal \npassage and temperatures cool with lows near or below freezing for \nmost locations across the Sandhills and southwest/western Nebraska. \nWesterly winds will increase to around 20 to 25 mph with gusts \nup to 50 mph. Over the last few model runs the system is coming \nin drier with limited moisture advecting ahead of the front, \nthus don't expect any precipitation with the system. However, \nwithout the aforemention moisture, there will be an increase in\nfire weather concerns as min RH will drop to around 20 to 30 \npercent across fire weather zones 210, 219, 204 and 206, as dry,\nwarm conditions combine with very strong winds in the \nafternoon. \n\nAs for Temperatures on Thursday will be in the 50s behind the cold \nfront. This \"cool down\" will be brief as temperatures bounce back on \nFriday as an upper level ridge centers over the High Plains Friday \nahead of the next system moving in Friday afternoon/evening. This \nsystem has continued to evolve over the past several days with the \nlatest few model runs now backing off on the closed low solution and \nboth models showing more consistency in a more open wave pattern. \nUncertainty still remains on the exact track of the system, but at \nthis time looks to at least have decent probability in seeing \nscattered to widespread precipitation with this system heading \ninto the weekend. At this time temperatures will be on the warm \nside expect precipitation to fall as rain Friday afternoon into \nearly evening, and could potentially even see some isolated \nthunderstorms. At temperatures cool overnight, there will be a \nlow end chance for precipitation to become a mix of rain/snow \nand even change briefly to all snow across the northwest \nSandhills. This system still remains offshore and could still \nevolve in the coming days, thus will need to continue to monitor\nthe evolution and track of the system, but latest guidance \ncontinue to prog the system approaching the central CONUS \nweaker than earlier model runs suggested. Cooler temperatures \nare expected for the weekend with highs only in the 40s across \nmuch of western and north central Nebraska both Saturday and \nSunday.",
